// `drop` takes an `UnsafePtr<T>` and destroys the pointee. The old `drop(value: place)` form accepted a
// place RAII already owned — a local, a by-value parameter — and the scope destructor then ran on the same
// object again: two destructor calls, a double free, no diagnostic. It shipped that way in six
// `sorted_map.kama` sites and crashed `SortedMap.put` on any owning value (`0.9.290`).
//
// ⚠️ This is a REMOVAL, not a check. A local is not a pointer, so the shape cannot be written at all —
// which is the point of taking the pointer rather than adding a rule. To end a local's life early, give it
// a scope: `{ T x = …; }`.
import { std::collections::DynamicArray };

type resource R {
    DynamicArray<int32> xs;
    ~R() { }
    public ctor make() { this.xs = DynamicArray.empty(); }
}

fn int32 main() {
    R local = R.make();
    drop(value: local);        // the old place form — gone
    return 0;
}
